          "children": [
            {
              "id": "108:784",
              "name": "Dear reader,",
              "w": 620,
              "h": 55,
              "text": "Dear reader,",
              "font": {
                "family": "Instrument Serif",
                "style": "Italic"
              },
              "size": 38,
              "link": null
            },
            {
              "id": "108:785",
              "name": "[What brought you here? Describe the event, your role, and the idea or question you explored in a short personal note.]",
              "w": 620,
              "h": 125,
              "text": "The NSF AI Spring School reshaped how I think about artificial intelligence. Sessions connecting human cognition, AI robustness, and user-facing security reminded me that building better AI starts with a better understanding of ourselves. The experience strengthened my interest in AI research and thoughtful, human-centered systems.",
              "font": {
                "family": "Inter",
                "style": "Regular",
                "variationSettings": {
                  "wght": 400,
                  "slnt": 0
                }
              },
              "size": 17,
              "link": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "108:787",
          "name": "Highlights",
          "w": 380,
          "h": 379,
          "layout": "VERTICAL",
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "108:788",
              "name": "The moments I’m keeping",
              "w": 380,
              "h": 55,
              "text": "The moments I’m keeping",
              "font": {
                "family": "Instrument Serif",
                "style": "Regular"
              },
              "size": 38,
              "link": null
            },
            {
              "id": "108:789",
              "name": "Dr. Cory Merkel’s “Breaking Brains” session explored ambiguity in human perception and robustness in neuromorphic systems. Dr. Maria Gorlatova discussed evaluating and securing XR experiences with large multimodal models. Birds-of-a-Feather discussions led by Dr. Amanda Fernandez connected me with peers, research opportunities, and new questions about HCI.",
              "w": 380,
              "h": 300,
              "text": "Dr. Cory Merkel’s “Breaking Brains” session explored ambiguity in human perception and robustness in neuromorphic systems.\n\nDr. Maria Gorlatova discussed evaluating and securing XR experiences with large multimodal models.\n\nBirds-of-a-Feather discussions led by            Dr. Amanda Fernandez connected me with peers, research opportunities, and new questions about HCI.",
